.TH SET_JOINQ 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_joinq \- Join all elements in a set into a single string, quoting elements that contain a separator

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/stralloc.h>
.IB #include <lib/set.h>
.IB #include <lib/byte.h>

.IB void
.IB set_joinq(const set_t* set, stralloc* out, char sep, char quote);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_joinq\fR function joins all elements in a set into a single string, quoting elements that contain a separator. It takes four arguments: the set, a stralloc to store the result, a separator, and a quote character. The function does not return a value.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB set
The set to join into a single string.
.TP
.IB out
A stralloc to store the result.
.TP
.IB sep
The separator to use to join the elements.
.TP
.IB quote
The quote character to use to quote elements that contain the separator.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_joinq\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/stralloc.h>
#include <lib/set.h>
#include <lib/byte.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    set_init(&set);

    set_inserts(&set, "apple");
    set_inserts(&set, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set, "cherry");

    stralloc out;
    stralloc_init(&out);

    set_joinq(&set, &out, ',', '"');

    printf("Joined string: %s\n", out.s);

    set_free(&set);
    stralloc_free(&out);
    return 0;
}
.nef